powerpagecomponent.content:
{ "source": "{% extends 'OT Layout' %}
{% block content %}
{% assign ot_product = settings['OT/ProductName'] | default: 'Outcome Testing' %}
{% comment %}
 Review detail: renders the questionnaire version issued to this review
 (PP-07, PP-08, PP-09).

 The chain is Checklist Version -> Section -> Question -> Question Version.
 Questions are read from the checklist version stamped on the review instance,
 and of each question's versions only the one in force on the review's
 reference day renders: the submission day once submitted, otherwise today
 (AD-015, BR-013, FR-030, FR-031). A retired version keeps its effective-to
 date and stays Active, so without that filter every version of a question
 rendered at once - which is how a submitted Tax review came to show Tax check
 reason three times and hold an answer on each. The server applies the same
 rule (ResponseRules.IsVersionEffective) to the submit gate and the outcome.

 Answerable by the assigned checker (AD-053). Both writes on this page go through an
 allowlisted trigger column and a synchronous plug-in rather than the Web API's own
 create/update/action verbs, for two different reasons. Answering PATCHes a JSON payload
 onto al_reviewinstance.al_answerrequest, because Power Pages refuses the al_response
 create's @odata.bind association - and a fail reason's $ref associate - with error
 90040106 regardless of table permissions; AnswerRequestPlugin parses the payload and
 AnswerWriter does the al_response create/update instead. Submitting PATCHes
 al_reviewinstance.al_submitrequested, because the Portals Web API cannot invoke the
 al_SubmitReview Custom API at all; SubmitRequestPlugin does that work instead of a Power
 Automate flow. Every rule that matters - the submission lock, the AD-023 column match,
 the option subset and AD-020 section ownership - is enforced by ResponseGuardPlugin, so
 nothing here is a security boundary (NFR-SEC-01).
{% endcomment %}

{% assign review_id = request.params['id'] %}

{% comment %}
 F48, fixed 2026-09-21. An id from the query string reaches a fetchxml `eq` on a
 uniqueidentifier column, and Dataverse throws on a value that is not a GUID - which this
 Liquid surfaces as `Liquid error: Exception has been thrown by the target of an
 invocation.` printed above the page's own empty state. Nothing leaked and the page still
 rendered, but a platform exception was put in front of a reader who can do nothing with
 it, and the friendly state that WAS reached says it better.

 Checked by shape rather than by asking Dataverse, because asking is the thing that
 throws. Dashes and the braced form are stripped, then every hex digit is removed: what
 survives must be empty and the hex must be exactly 32 characters.

 An id that fails the check is treated as no id at all, so the page falls into the state it
 already has for arriving without one: the claim-by-case path, and failing that "no review selected". That is deliberate, over a message of
 its own - a reader who mistyped a link and a reader probing for ids are shown the same
 thing, so neither learns whether an id was malformed or simply unknown. A well-formed id
 that matches no row is untouched by any of this.
{% endcomment %}
{% assign ot_id_hex = review_id | downcase | replace: '-','' | replace: '{','' | replace: '}','' %}
{% assign ot_id_len = ot_id_hex | size %}
{% assign ot_id_rest = ot_id_hex | replace: '0','' | replace: '1','' | replace: '2','' | replace: '3','' | replace: '4','' | replace: '5','' | replace: '6','' | replace: '7','' | replace: '8','' | replace: '9','' | replace: 'a','' | replace: 'b','' | replace: 'c','' | replace: 'd','' | replace: 'e','' | replace: 'f','' %}
{% assign ot_id_ok = false %}
{% if ot_id_len == 32 and ot_id_rest == '' %}{% assign ot_id_ok = true %}{% endif %}
{% unless ot_id_ok %}{% assign review_id = '' %}{% endunless %}

{% comment %}
 A claim lands here by case rather than by review id: the queue page cannot read the new
 instance's id over the Web API (the al_reviewinstance allowlist carries only the two
 trigger columns, and widening it would hand the reviewer the status column), but Liquid
 can, with no allowlist in the way. The open instance of that discipline assigned to the
 signed-in user is the one just created; if none is found the page reads as "no review
 selected", which is what a stale link should say.
{% endcomment %}
{% if review_id == null or review_id == empty %}
 {% assign case_param = request.params['case'] %}
 {% comment %}
 F48, fixed 2026-09-21. An id from the query string reaches a fetchxml `eq` on a
 uniqueidentifier column, and Dataverse throws on a value that is not a GUID - which this
 Liquid surfaces as `Liquid error: Exception has been thrown by the target of an
 invocation.` printed above the page's own empty state. Nothing leaked and the page still
 rendered, but a platform exception was put in front of a reader who can do nothing with
 it, and the friendly state that WAS reached says it better.

 Checked by shape rather than by asking Dataverse, because asking is the thing that
 throws. Dashes and the braced form are stripped, then every hex digit is removed: what
 survives must be empty and the hex must be exactly 32 characters.

 An id that fails the check is treated as no id at all, so the page falls into the state it
 already has for arriving without one: "no review selected". That is deliberate, over a message of
 its own - a reader who mistyped a link and a reader probing for ids are shown the same
 thing, so neither learns whether an id was malformed or simply unknown. A well-formed id
 that matches no row is untouched by any of this.
 {% endcomment %}
 {% assign ot_id_hex = case_param | downcase | replace: '-','' | replace: '{','' | replace: '}','' %}
 {% assign ot_id_len = ot_id_hex | size %}
 {% assign ot_id_rest = ot_id_hex | replace: '0','' | replace: '1','' | replace: '2','' | replace: '3','' | replace: '4','' | replace: '5','' | replace: '6','' | replace: '7','' | replace: '8','' | replace: '9','' | replace: 'a','' | replace: 'b','' | replace: 'c','' | replace: 'd','' | replace: 'e','' | replace: 'f','' %}
 {% assign ot_id_ok = false %}
 {% if ot_id_len == 32 and ot_id_rest == '' %}{% assign ot_id_ok = true %}{% endif %}
 {% unless ot_id_ok %}{% assign case_param = '' %}{% endunless %}
 {% assign type_param = request.params['type'] %}
 {% if user and case_param and case_param != empty %}
 {% fetchxml by_case %}
 <fetch top="1">
 <entity name="al_reviewinstance">
 <attribute name="al_reviewinstanceid" />
 <filter type="and">
 <condition attribute="al_outcomecaseid" operator="eq" value="{{ case_param | xml_escape }}" />
 <condition attribute="al_assignedcontactid" operator="eq" value="{{ user.id }}" />
 <condition attribute="al_submittedon" operator="null" />
 {% if type_param and type_param != empty %}
 <condition attribute="al_reviewtype" operator="eq" value="{{ type_param | xml_escape }}" />
 {% endif %}
 </filter>
 <order attribute="createdon" descending="true" />
 </entity>
 </fetch>
 {% endfetchxml %}
 {% if by_case.results and by_case.results.entities.size > 0 %}
 {% assign by_case_row = by_case.results.entities[0] %}
 {% assign review_id = by_case_row.al_reviewinstanceid %}
 {% endif %}
 {% endif %}
{% endif %}

{% if review_id == null or review_id == empty %}
 {% include 'OT Empty State' title: 'No review selected' message: 'Open a review from a worklist to see its questionnaire.' %}
{% else %}

 {% fetchxml review %}
 <fetch top="1">
 <entity name="al_reviewinstance">
 <attribute name="al_reviewinstanceid" />
 <attribute name="al_name" />
 <attribute name="al_reviewtype" />
 <attribute name="al_reviewstatus" />
 <attribute name="al_sequence" />
 <attribute name="al_startedon" />
 <attribute name="al_submittedon" />
 <attribute name="al_checklistversionid" />
 <attribute name="al_assignedcontactid" />
 <attribute name="al_pendingaccountability" />
 <attribute name="al_pendingremedialactions" />
 <filter type="and">
 <condition attribute="al_reviewinstanceid" operator="eq" value="{{ review_id | xml_escape }}" />
 </filter>
 <link-entity name="al_outcomecase" from="al_outcomecaseid" to="al_outcomecaseid" link-type="outer" alias="case">
 <attribute name="al_outcomecaseid" />
 <attribute name="al_casereference" />
 <attribute name="al_ioreference" />
 <attribute name="al_clientname" />
 <attribute name="al_advisername" />
 <attribute name="al_adviseremail" />
 <attribute name="al_adviserstatus" />
 <attribute name="al_paraplanner" />
 <attribute name="al_paraplanneremail" />
 <attribute name="al_products" />
 <attribute name="al_casetype" />
 <attribute name="al_advicedate" />
 <attribute name="al_productsolutiontype" />
 <attribute name="al_producttypeid" />
 <attribute name="al_samplesourceid" />
 <attribute name="al_casetypeid" />
 <attribute name="al_preorpostcheckid" />
 <attribute name="al_samplesource" />
 <attribute name="al_taxcheckername" />
 <attribute name="al_aqscheckername" />
 <attribute name="al_checkdate" />
 <attribute name="al_preorpostcheck" />
 <attribute name="al_vulnerableclient" />
 <attribute name="al_vulnerableclientid" />
 <attribute name="al_taxcheckrequired" />
 <attribute name="al_taxteamdisposition" />
 <attribute name="al_checklistitems" />
 <attribute name="al_reviewrouteid" />
 </link-entity>
 </entity>
 </fetch>
 {% endfetchxml %}

 {% comment %}
 The size test has to come FIRST. Indexing entities[0] on an empty result throws
 "Index was outside the bounds of the array" on this engine, and the index is evaluated
 before the null test below - so without this guard the "Review not available" message,
 which exists for precisely the case of a review you cannot see, was unreachable. The
 same pattern the tiles on OT My Work already use.
 {% endcomment %}
 {% if review.results and review.results.entities.size > 0 %}
 {% assign rv = review.results.entities[0] %}
 {% endif %}

 {% if rv == null %}
 {% include 'OT Empty State' title: 'Review not available' message: 'This review does not exist, or you do not have permission to view it.' %}
 {% else %}

 {% assign cv_id = rv.al_checklistversionid.id %}

 {% comment %}
 The review's discipline, resolved once. 120910006 reads its third option differently
 on a Tax review (AD-055 amended), and both paths that draw that scale - the grid
 headings below and the OT Answer Options include - have to make the same reading. Held
 in one variable so they cannot drift apart, which is how the inline path came to draw
 an AQS question as a tax check.
 {% endcomment %}
 {% assign is_tax_review = false %}
 {% if rv.al_reviewtype.value == 120910200 %}{% assign is_tax_review = true %}{% endif %}

 {% comment %}
 AD-020 section ownership, keyed by the review's discipline. Filtering in the query
 rather than in the loop is what makes the Tax/AQS split structural: a Tax checker's
 page never receives an AQS section's questions at all, which is what PP-08's
 "no cross-discipline overwrite" needs. The same rule is enforced again server-side
 by ResponseGuardPlugin, because a filter in a page is not a security boundary
 (NFR-SEC-01).
 {% endcomment %}
 {% if rv.al_reviewtype.value == 120910200 %}
 {% assign owner_role = 120910100 %}
 {% else %}
 {% assign owner_role = 120910101 %}
 {% endif %}

 {% comment %}
 A submitted review is read-only (PP-11). The questionnaire renders for a submitted
 review too, so every control is disabled and the autosave script is not emitted at
 all. ResponseGuardPlugin refuses the write regardless; disabling the control is what
 stops a checker typing into a field that can never save.
 {% endcomment %}
 {% if rv.al_submittedon %}
 {% assign locked = true %}
 {% else %}
 {% assign locked = false %}
 {% endif %}

 {% comment %}
 The case header does NOT freeze when the Tax check is submitted (project owner,
 2026-09-23: "both teams need to be able to edit the headers").

 It did for one day. On 2026-09-22 this page ran a second fetch for a submitted Tax
 review on the case and forced `header_locked` true when it found one, so an AQS
 checker picking the case up after the handover read the Tax checker's header but could
 not correct it. That is reversed, and the fetch is gone with it - a query whose answer
 no longer changes anything is a query to delete, not to leave wired to a variable
 nothing reads.

 So the header follows `locked` alone: read-only exactly when THIS review is submitted
 (PP-11), and editable by whichever team holds the open review otherwise. That matches
 what the server now does - CaseHeaderRequestPlugin asks EnsureAssignedToCase and
 nothing else about the ordinary fields - and keeping the two readings identical is
 what stops the page offering an edit the server refuses, or refusing one it would
 allow.

 The Tax team's own two fields are the exception and are handled where they are drawn,
 below: they re-derive the review route (BR-004, AD-036), so EnsureTaxReviewOpen goes
 on refusing them once the Tax check is submitted.
 {% endcomment %}
 {% assign header_locked = locked %}

 {% comment %}
 AD-056: a reviewer reads every review but changes only their own. Because reading
 another reviewer's review is now intended, the page must say so and disable its
 controls - otherwise a checker types into fields that the table permissions will
 refuse on save. The permission chain is the actual boundary; this is what stops the
 user discovering it the hard way.
 {% endcomment %}
 {% if rv.al_assignedcontactid.id and rv.al_assignedcontactid.id == user.id %}
 {% assign mine = true %}
 {% else %}
 {% assign mine = false %}
 {% endif %}

 {% if locked or mine == false %}
 {% assign editable = false %}
 {% else %}
 {% assign editable = true %}
 {% endif %}

 <section class="ot-card" aria-labelledby="ot-review-summary">
 <h2 class="ot-card__title" id="ot-review-summary">
 {{ rv.al_reviewtype.label | escape }} review
 <span data-ot-review-status>{% include 'OT Status Badge' status: rv.al_reviewstatus.label %}</span>
 </h2>
 <dl class="ot-dl">
 <div>
 <dt>Case</dt>
 <dd>
 {% assign cid = rv['case.al_outcomecaseid'] %}
 {% if cid %}
 <a href="/case-details?id={{ cid }}">{{ rv['case.al_casereference'] | default: 'Untitled case' | escape }}</a>
 {% else %}
 {{ rv['case.al_casereference'] | default: '—' | escape }}
 {% endif %}
 </dd>
 </div>
 <div><dt>Client</dt><dd>{{ rv['case.al_clientname'] | default: '—' | escape }}</dd></div>
 <div><dt>Adviser</dt><dd>{{ rv['case.al_advisername'] | default: '—' | escape }}</dd></div>
 <div><dt>Checklist version</dt><dd>{{ rv.al_checklistversionid.name | default: '—' | escape }}</dd></div>
 <div><dt>Sequence</dt><dd>{{ rv.al_sequence | default: '—' }}</dd></div>
 <div><dt>Started</dt><dd>{{ rv.al_startedon | date: 'dd MMM yyyy' | default: '—' }}</dd></div>
 <div><dt>Submitted</dt><dd data-ot-submitted-on>{{ rv.al_submittedon | date: 'dd MMM yyyy' | default: '—' }}</dd></div>
 </dl>

 {% comment %}
 Keeping a copy of the checklist. The browser's print dialogue is the export - every
 platform's offers "Save as PDF" - and the print rules in outcome-testing.css strip
 the chrome so what lands in the file is this page's questionnaire and answers.

 No PDF library, for two reasons: Power Pages serves scripts only from an allowlisted
 set of CDNs, and a generated document would be a second description of this layout to
 keep in step with the one people read. The button itself is .ot-btn, which the print
 rules hide, so it never appears in the output.

 Offered on a submitted review only. What this exports is a record of the check, and
 before submission there is no check to record - the answers are still being edited,
 so a file saved from here would be a draft that reads exactly like a final one and
 carries no submission date to say otherwise. The button appears the moment the
 review is submitted and the page turns read-only.
 {% endcomment %}
 {% if rv.al_submittedon %}
 <p class="ot-print-action">
 <button type="button" class="ot-btn" data-ot-print>Save as PDF</button>
 </p>

 <script>
 (function () {
 'use strict';
 var button = document.querySelector('[data-ot-print]');
 if (!button) { return; }
 button.addEventListener('click', function () { window.print(); });
 })();
 </script>
 {% endif %}
 </section>

 {% if rv.al_submittedon %}
 <div class="ot-notice" role="status">
 <strong>Submitted {{ rv.al_submittedon | date: 'dd MMM yyyy' }}.</strong>
 This review is locked. If it needs changing, only a T&amp;C Manager can reopen or regrade it,
 with a recorded reason.
 </div>
 {% elsif mine == false %}
 {% comment %}
 Reading another reviewer's work is intended under AD-056; changing it is not.
 Saying so plainly is better than presenting controls that fail on save.
 {% endcomment %}
 <div class="ot-notice" role="status">
 <strong>This review is assigned to someone else.</strong>
 You can read it, but only the assigned reviewer can record or change answers on it.
 </div>
 {% endif %}

 {% comment %}
 Filled in by the autosave script when it can tell the page is behind what was saved.
 Empty and hidden otherwise, so a reviewer whose page is current never sees it. The
 CACHE_LAG comment on the script says why the page can be behind at all.
 {% endcomment %}
 <div class="ot-notice" role="status" data-ot-cache-notice hidden></div>

 {% comment %}
 The Tax check's notes (project owner, 2026-09-19).

 On a submitted review only, and only where this review is not itself the Tax one.
 The Tax review renders Q-TAX-03 in its own questionnaire already, so repeating it
 above the form would show the same answer twice; what was missing is the AQS
 checker's view of it, which AD-020 filters out of their form by design.

 NOT gated on this review's own submission date (F49, fixed 2026-09-21).
 It was, and `rv` is the review being VIEWED - so the panel appeared on an AQS review
 only once the AQS checker had already submitted it, which is after the only moment it
 could have changed their assessment. The condition was guarding the right thing on the
 wrong review: what must be submitted is the TAX check whose words these are, and
 'OT Tax Notes' already enforces exactly that in its own fetch
 (al_reviewtype eq 120910200 AND al_submittedon not-null). So the protection the old
 comment described is kept in full, and it is now applied to the check it was about.

 This matters more from 2026-09-20, when a Tax fail began being carried into ONE
 combined remediation raised after the AQS check: that makes the AQS checker the person
 the Tax checker is writing for, and they need it while the form is still open.
 {% endcomment %}
 {% if rv.al_reviewtype.value != 120910200 %}
 {% include 'OT Tax Notes' case_id: rv['case.al_outcomecaseid'] %}
 {% endif %}

 {% comment %}
 The document's header block (AD-098): the case fields in the order the Checker
 Checklist lays them out. Outcome Case columns captured at intake, not questions; read
 only here, as on the checklist itself. Choice columns come through the alias as
 option objects, so their label is read rather than their value.
 {% endcomment %}
 {% assign h_adviserstatus = rv['case.al_adviserstatus'] %}
 {% assign h_casetype = rv['case.al_casetype'] %}
 {% assign h_producttype = rv['case.al_productsolutiontype'] %}
 {% comment %}
 Product / solution type is a managed LIST: its options are al_listoption rows the
 checking team maintains on the Code App's Dropdown options page, not choice metadata a
 developer deploys (AD-187). h_producttype above is the choice column the case was
 imported with and is still read for display, because a case that has not been edited
 since the migration carries only that.
 {% endcomment %}
 {% assign h_producttypeid = rv['case.al_producttypeid'] %}
 {% assign held_producttype = h_producttypeid.id %}
 {% assign h_samplesourceid = rv['case.al_samplesourceid'] %}
 {% assign held_samplesource = h_samplesourceid.id %}
 {% assign h_casetypeid = rv['case.al_casetypeid'] %}
 {% assign held_casetype = h_casetypeid.id %}
 {% assign h_preorpostcheckid = rv['case.al_preorpostcheckid'] %}
 {% assign held_preorpost = h_preorpostcheckid.id %}
 {% assign h_vulnerableclientid = rv['case.al_vulnerableclientid'] %}
 {% assign held_vulnerable = h_vulnerableclientid.id %}
 {% comment %}
 TODAY, not the review's as_of. as_of answers "which question version was this review
 answered against", which is a question about history; this answers "what may be chosen
 now", which is a question about the present. A submitted review's header is not
 editable at all, so there is no case where the two should agree.
 {% endcomment %}
 {% assign product_day = now | date: 'yyyy-MM-dd' %}
 {% comment %}
 The six option lists below feed the editable header only (the header_locked == false
 branch further down), so a submitted review, whose header is text, does not read them.
 {% endcomment %}
 {% unless header_locked %}
 {% fetchxml product_types %}
 <fetch>
 <entity name="al_listoption">
 <attribute name="al_listoptionid" />
 <attribute name="al_name" />
 <attribute name="al_sortorder" />
 <filter type="and">
 <condition attribute="statecode" operator="eq" value="0" />
 <condition attribute="al_list" operator="eq" value="120910840" />
 <filter type="or">
 <condition attribute="al_effectivefrom" operator="null" />
 <condition attribute="al_effectivefrom" operator="on-or-before" value="{{ product_day }}" />
 </filter>
 <filter type="or">
 <condition attribute="al_effectiveto" operator="null" />
 <condition attribute="al_effectiveto" operator="gt" value="{{ product_day }}" />
 </filter>
 </filter>
 <order attribute="al_sortorder" />
 <order attribute="al_name" />
 </entity>
 </fetch>
 {% endfetchxml %}
{% fetchxml sample_sources %}
 <fetch>
 <entity name="al_listoption">
 <attribute name="al_listoptionid" />
 <attribute name="al_name" />
 <attribute name="al_sortorder" />
 <filter type="and">
 <condition attribute="statecode" operator="eq" value="0" />
 <condition attribute="al_list" operator="eq" value="120910841" />
 <filter type="or">
 <condition attribute="al_effectivefrom" operator="null" />
 <condition attribute="al_effectivefrom" operator="on-or-before" value="{{ product_day }}" />
 </filter>
 <filter type="or">
 <condition attribute="al_effectiveto" operator="null" />
 <condition attribute="al_effectiveto" operator="gt" value="{{ product_day }}" />
 </filter>
 </filter>
 <order attribute="al_sortorder" />
 <order attribute="al_name" />
 </entity>
 </fetch>
 {% endfetchxml %}
{% fetchxml vulnerable_clients %}
 <fetch>
 <entity name="al_listoption">
 <attribute name="al_listoptionid" />
 <attribute name="al_name" />
 <attribute name="al_sortorder" />
 <filter type="and">
 <condition attribute="statecode" operator="eq" value="0" />
 <condition attribute="al_list" operator="eq" value="120910845" />
 <filter type="or">
 <condition attribute="al_effectivefrom" operator="null" />
 <condition attribute="al_effectivefrom" operator="on-or-before" value="{{ product_day }}" />
 </filter>
 <filter type="or">
 <condition attribute="al_effectiveto" operator="null" />
 <condition attribute="al_effectiveto" operator="gt" value="{{ product_day }}" />
 </filter>
 </filter>
 <order attribute="al_sortorder" />
 <order attribute="al_name" />
 </entity>
 </fetch>
 {% endfetchxml %}
{% fetchxml case_types %}
 <fetch>
 <entity name="al_listoption">
 <attribute name="al_listoptionid" />
 <attribute name="al_name" />
 <attribute name="al_sortorder" />
 <filter type="and">
 <condition attribute="statecode" operator="eq" value="0" />
 <condition attribute="al_list" operator="eq" value="120910842" />
 <filter type="or">
 <condition attribute="al_effectivefrom" operator="null" />
 <condition attribute="al_effectivefrom" operator="on-or-before" value="{{ product_day }}" />
 </filter>
 <filter type="or">
 <condition attribute="al_effectiveto" operator="null" />
 <condition attribute="al_effectiveto" operator="gt" value="{{ product_day }}" />
 </filter>
 </filter>
 <order attribute="al_sortorder" />
 <order attribute="al_name" />
 </entity>
 </fetch>
 {% endfetchxml %}
{% fetchxml pre_post_checks %}
 <fetch>
 <entity name="al_listoption">
 <attribute name="al_listoptionid" />
 <attribute name="al_name" />
 <attribute name="al_sortorder" />
 <filter type="and">
 <condition attribute="statecode" operator="eq" value="0" />
 <condition attribute="al_list" operator="eq" value="120910843" />
 <filter type="or">
 <condition attribute="al_effectivefrom" operator="null" />
 <condition attribute="al_effectivefrom" operator="on-or-before" value="{{ product_day }}" />
 </filter>
 <filter type="or">
 <condition attribute="al_effectiveto" operator="null" />
 <condition attribute="al_effectiveto" operator="gt" value="{{ product_day }}" />
 </filter>
 </filter>
 <order attribute="al_sortorder" />
 <order attribute="al_name" />
 </entity>
 </fetch>
 {% endfetchxml %}

{% comment %}
 Products is a SET: a case covers several, which is what the free-text column it replaces
 already did ("Product(s)", seeded as "Pension; ISA"). So it is a many-to-many rather than a
 lookup, and needs two reads - the options on offer, and the ones this case already carries.
{% endcomment %}
{% comment %}
 count is explicit because the catalogue is now 55 products (project owner, 2026-09-23) and
 a fetch that does not say how many it wants takes the platform's default page. A list
 silently cut at its page size looks exactly like a list that is complete, and the product
 a checker cannot find is the one they stop looking for.
{% endcomment %}
{% fetchxml products %}
<fetch count="200">
 <entity name="al_listoption">
 <attribute name="al_listoptionid" />
 <attribute name="al_name" />
 <attribute name="al_sortorder" />
 <filter type="and">
 <condition attribute="statecode" operator="eq" value="0" />
 <condition attribute="al_list" operator="eq" value="120910844" />
 <filter type="or">
 <condition attribute="al_effectivefrom" operator="null" />
 <condition attribute="al_effectivefrom" operator="on-or-before" value="{{ product_day }}" />
 </filter>
 <filter type="or">
 <condition attribute="al_effectiveto" operator="null" />
 <condition attribute="al_effectiveto" operator="gt" value="{{ product_day }}" />
 </filter>
 </filter>
 <order attribute="al_sortorder" />
 <order attribute="al_name" />
 </entity>
</fetch>
{% endfetchxml %}
 {% endunless %}

{% fetchxml case_products %}
<fetch>
 <entity name="al_listoption">
 <attribute name="al_listoptionid" />
 <attribute name="al_name" />
 <link-entity name="al_listoption_al_outcomecase_products" from="al_listoptionid" to="al_listoptionid" intersect="true">
 <filter type="and">
 <condition attribute="al_outcomecaseid" operator="eq" value="{{ rv['case.al_outcomecaseid'] | xml_escape }}" />
 </filter>
 </link-entity>
 </entity>
</fetch>
{% endfetchxml %}

{% comment %}
 Pipe-delimited membership tests, the way OT Answer Options builds `locked`: delimited
 rather than compared as a bare substring, because no guid is a substring of another today
 and a test that depends on that staying true is one that breaks quietly.
{% endcomment %}
{% assign products_any = false %}
{% assign offered_product_ids = '|' %}
